{
 "id": "2026-karaca-ornamental",
 "name": "Ornamental Pools: Kansei semantic differential of 8 designs",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: eight conceptual design images of an ornamental pool on a lawn in front of the same apartment blocks, made with 3D modelling and image editing software from the paper's Taguchi L8 orthogonal array of five two-level design factors: depth (shallow, deep), interior surface colour (dark, light), surface planting (bare, vegetated), form (natural, geometric) and motion (still, dynamic fountains). Participants say whether they are a landscape design student or faculty member, then see the eight pools one at a time and rate each on the paper's six Kansei word pairs on a 7-point semantic differential scale: stress inducing - calming, mentally fatiguing - mentally restorative, confusing - legible, boring - captivating, aesthetically displeasing - aesthetically pleasing, unsatisfying - satisfying (1 = left word, 7 = right word, so higher is more positive, as in the paper). Rebuilt from Karaca & Perçin (2026), Integrating Kansei Engineering into Sustainable Landscape Design: An Empirical Study on Ornamental Pools, Sustainability 18(14): 6954, with the images cropped from its Figure 1 (captions trimmed) and the word pairs, factors and design matrix from its Tables 1-3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: exp1-exp8 as numbered in Table 3; answers are in kansei, keyed by image id; the factor levels of each pool are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean score of each pool on each word pair, Taguchi signal-to-noise ratios (larger is better) to find the best level of each factor for each word, and ANOVA of the factors' contributions; the paper (91 participants: 71 third- and fourth-year landscape design students and 20 landscape design faculty members) found surface planting the dominant factor for captivating and legible, motion the key factor for mental restoration, and a shallow, light-coloured, vegetated, natural-form pool with dynamic water as the optimal configuration, evoking captivating, aesthetically pleasing and satisfying responses. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper briefed participants on the study and gave definitions of each word, which it does not print, so none are given here; (2) the paper ran the survey with three groups at different times, varying the order of the pools between groups; here the order is randomized for each participant; (3) the background question and the instruction text are ours, and anyone can take part, while the paper required training in landscape design; (4) the images are the figure copies (about 870 px wide).",
